    "turn_idx": 133,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, yeah. No, he will. I mean, we did this. I mean, the thing first comes to mind is this thing called the Justice Mental Health Collaboration Act. And it's about It's about mental health and the criminal justice system. And first of all, there's too many people in prison because they have mental illness and being in prison ain't gonna help their mental illness. So we have these things called mental health courts. And when the arresting officer and the prosecutor and the judge and the defense attorney decide this person belongs in mental health court. Then that's where they go. And instead of going to prison, they get treatment and housing. And we do this thing called part of this was crisis intervention training, which is training for law enforcement to understand, to be able to identify when a mental health issue is energizing a situation, to identify when someone, when what's happening, and this prevents one, police from killing people, and also it helps their safety too, and you can de-escalate a situation if you have this training. I asked a Columbia Heights, Minnesota, the suburb of Minneapolis, I went to their whole foresthead crisis intervention training, and I said, can someone just tell me, the police chief said that the day after he got the training, he didn't kill someone, he would have otherwise killed. And I said, can someone give me a more garden variety story? So this police woman said to me, okay, I just pointed to her and she said, okay, garden variety story. A few months ago, about four months ago, I was out on the street, and I heard a woman screaming, and I thought it was a domestic",
