    "turn_idx": 232,
    "turn_text": "Um, I'm not a fan of Trump's. He came to Minnesota. For the first time, he did a rally in Minnesota on the Sunday before the election. And he came, he landed his plane at the Minneapolis, St. Paul airport, and he trashed the Somali community in Minnesota. And he said to the crowd there, you've suffered enough. And he just didn't know what he was talking about. And so, I talk in this last chapter, it's about being as good as the people we represent. And I talk about Wilmer, Minnesota, which is in south central Minnesota. It's an ag town. Minnesota is the largest turkey producing state in the country. And Wilmer is a county sea for Canada, Yohai County, which is the largest turkey producing county in Minnesota. is in Wilmer. And the year and a half before, about a year before, I was in my office in DC and there was a new, one of my staff came in and said, there's a new class of pages. And we have these Senate pages, they're all juniors in high school. And we have 30 in each class, so we don't usually necessarily have a Minnesota page. And I said, but there's a Minnesota page, and she's a Somali Minnesota girl from Wilmer. And her name is Munad Lahi. And her principal had recommended her. And she did, she interviewed with our staff in Minnesota, and she was terrific. And she wrote a great essay. And so she's here. She's here. So I said, well, let's go down, and I want to go down and meet her. So I go down to the center floor, and the pages are all there. And it's easy to pick her out. She's Somali, and she's wearing her hijab, her head scarf, over her page uniform. So I go up to her, and I say, you look like a Minnesotan. And she smiled. And I got to know Moona. And she was a really unbelievably great young woman, young lady. And when the Somali ambassador came to Capitol Hill to meet with members of Congress, with Somali populations in their state, I made sure to bring her so that he could see that a Somali Minnesota girl was a page in the United States Senate. So in June of 2016, during this ugly, ugly election note, Moona was graduating. She was a senior, and she was chosen to be the class speaker. And so I invited myself to the commencement so that I could introduce her. So I go to Wilmer, and during this toxic campaign, this awful campaign year is awful time. This was the best two and a half hours I spent the whole time. So I get there early, and we have some coffee and donuts for people who are involved in the thing, and Moona gives me a big hug. And so she is the class speaker. The valedictorian is a girl named Maita Mera Meran. She was born in Ecuador. She is the valedictorian. The class president is Tate Hovland. I asked him what is, he was half Norwegian, half German, which is like 60% of the class was half Scandinavia and half German. And when I got to the sit on the stage, like we're sitting, looking at the program, they're 243 graduates. And about 60% were the Minnesota Scandinavian German. About 15, 20% Latino. about 15% Somali. And these kids loved each other. And there was a center aisle, so they have a really good orchestra at this place. It's not a big, terribly big town. They have a great orchestra, and there's a playing pomp and circumstance. The graduates come down. and Munna Abdullahi, ABD, she's first in the alphabet. And they come down and break like that. So Munna's coming down with one of the Carlson twins, with Michelle Carlson, and they're holding hands. And Michelle is just giving off this She and her identical sister Mary are just these unbelievable, you could see, they had asterisks next to their name, you honors, you had one asterisk two, you know, high honors three were highest honors. Both the Carlson twins were highest honors. And they both gave off this aura, just this positivity and joy. And her sister Mary was behind with holding hands with a Somali boy. And they came down like that. It was just beautiful. Tate gave his speech, got a huge applause. Maita gave her speech, it was great, she got a big applause. I went out, I introduced Moona, and she got this huge ovation. She gave her speech. She was great. She got a standing ovation. They played the battle hymn of the Republic every year. That's something they do. They have a great chorus too. It was the most stirring battle hymn of the Republic I ever heard. My job was to give out, not to give out the plums, but take a picture with each one. So I'm standing there ready to do that. And they make an announcement before they give the plums, they're saying like, wait until we say all 243 before plotting. And they go, OK, first, Munad Blahi. The place goes nuts. Just goes nuts. People stomping on the bleachers. And they did it for every kid. So, that's June of 16. November, election day, I go to the University of Minnesota. Moon is at the University of Minnesota. There are 40,000 students at the University of Minnesota. I'm going there to get out the boat. I run in the moon. She tells me that her sister, Anissa, was voter homecoming queen. So we lose the election. We have the election. I'm back in DC. The French ambassador, the ambassador from France, the United States, is in my office. And I said, who do the French consider a Frenchman? She said, well, Basically, a lot of Frenchmen consider a Frenchman, someone who can point back centuries, several centuries, to the village that their family came from. And I said, and they don't consider immigrants Frenchmen. You go, no. No, they don't. And I said, you pretty much isolate your immigrants. I said, yeah. And I thought about Paris. I thought about Belgium. And the thing about our country, as except for American Indians, we're all immigrants. We are all immigrants. And that's our strength as a country. That's our strength. There, they segregate immigrants and refugees. In the United States, we both am homecoming",
