    "turn_text": "I don't know, but there has been permission to do this and it's an ugly, ugly strain in America. That's always kind of been here, right? And is part of our original sin and there are people who are mad for economic reasons that they, and look, I grew up in the 50s. My dad didn't graduate high school. He was a printing salesman. We grew up, my brother and me and my parents, and we were in a two-bedroom, one-bath house. I felt like I was luckiest kid in the world because I was growing up middle class at the height of the middle class in America, in Minnesota, in St. Louis Park. And I just felt like I could do anything I wanted. And I think at that time, Americans especially white Americans felt it was their birthright that their kids would do better than, and now they don't feel that way. And there are some people that feel like, oh, these immigrants and other people, they're being handed a place in front of me. And I think that, you know, the reason I wrote, and part of the way I deal with Trump in this, I didn't want to write the Trump administration until my book was locked. I wanted to write about, what I wrote about was how this happened, how this happened. And part of it is, and the reason I wrote Rush Limbaugh was a big fat idiot and lies and lying liars who tell him was about lying. But it was about right-wing radio, Rush Limbaugh, and it was about Fox News, and they sued me. Bill O'Reilly sued me when lies and lying liars who tell them a fair and balanced look at the right came out, they sued me. It was just a misunderstanding. Bill O'Reilly didn't understand that in America, satire is protected speech even if the object of the satire doesn't get it. And so then what I didn't get the write a book about is the right-wing internet and Breitbart and that stuff. And there was permission given to this. And Fox gave it, and right-wing radio gave it, and the internet gave it. And we have self-segmented where people get all their information. from places that confirm their bias.",
